{
  "$schema": "./node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "hlekk-enwiki",
  // The demo at scale: English Wikipedia, namespaces 0 and 14, under the link-graph placement.
  //
  // WHY A FOURTH DEPLOYMENT, AND ONLY ONE PLACEMENT. The three simplewiki deployments answer the
  // demo's question -- a key derived from the link graph reads a page from 10.7 hosts of 43 where a
  // title hash needs 34.7, and a page's first category only gets to 26.2. That comparison needs
  // three databases loaded from identical records, which at this size costs ~22 hours each. So the
  // comparison stays at simplewiki, where it is complete and cheap to re-run, and this exists to
  // show the same key holding up two orders of magnitude further out.
  //
  // WHAT IS DELIBERATELY NOT HERE: no HLEKK_ALT, so nothing forwards to it and nothing forwards out;
  // no HLEKK_WIPE, so it carries no route that destroys 200 GB.
  "main": "examples/wiki/wiki-app.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-10-01",
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [
      { "name": "HLEKK_HOSTS", "class_name": "HlekkHostDO" },
      { "name": "HLEKK_DIRECTORY", "class_name": "HlekkDirectoryDO" }
    ]
  },
  // Its own namespaces. `enwiki/h-0` here is a different object from that name anywhere else.
  "migrations": [{ "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["HlekkHostDO", "HlekkDirectoryDO"] }],
  // Shared with the simplewiki deployments: a chunk key is `m/${database}/${index}`, and the
  // database names differ.
  "kv_namespaces": [{ "binding": "HLEKK_MAP", "id": "af144fc56d0541699307352f0a63ab76" }],
  "vars": {
    "HLEKK_DATABASE": "enwiki-graph",
    /**
     * 256, where simplewiki used 128, and `/admin/init?hosts=20&indexVshards=128` is the matching
     * call. Read once, when the directory first opens, so this is chosen before the load and cannot
     * be changed afterwards -- which is why it is sized from the prepared import and not a guess.
     *
     * `init` spreads these 256 across hosts at `per = ceil(256/20) = 13`, which gives 20 hosts, then
     * opens a second batch of 256 and takes the first 128 of it as index vshards. So: 512 vshards,
     * 384 of them taking data, 128 serving the unique title index, on 20 hosts.
     *
     * THE SIZING IS A HOST-SIZE TARGET, AND THE HOST COUNT IS THE ONLY KNOB LEFT. The prepared
     * import is 21,963,892 pages and 1,776,368,606 edges, which at the 61.4 bytes per stored row
     * simplewiki measured under all three placements is 102.8 GiB. Host count is quantised by
     * `per = ceil(256/ask)`: 20, 22, 26, 32 and 43 are reachable and nothing between them is.
     *
     * 20 puts the average host at 5.14 GiB. The spread around that average comes from the
     * partitioner and not from the vshard split -- a host holds one part, and `splitWithinHosts`
     * only chooses which of that host's own vshards each page lands on. The partitioner balances
     * parts by degree and holds the heaviest to the `--eps` ceiling of 1.20x the mean; at 20 parts
     * over this graph it left the lightest at 0.63x. So the hosts arrive between 3.3 and 6.2 GiB.
     * `DEFAULT_THRESHOLDS.bytes` sheds at 8 GB and a write meets SQLITE_FULL at 10: the heaviest
     * host keeps 1.8 GiB against the first and 3.8 against the second, which is the margin for a
     * row costing more here than the 61.4 bytes it cost two orders of magnitude down.
     *
     * Why not fewer, when fewer would fill them better. 18 would average 5.71 GiB and 16 would
     * average 6.43, both still under the shed line at 1.20x. The argument for 20 is asymmetric
     * risk: the only uncertain number is bytes per row, being wrong low costs nothing but empty
     * hosts, and being wrong high at 16 hosts puts the heaviest host within 0.3 GiB of shedding
     * mid-load. Why not more: 43 would load in about half the time, because the loader keeps one
     * batch in flight per host and pass B measured 136k edges/s at that width -- but it would leave
     * every host a quarter full, which demonstrates the sizing rather than exercises it.
     *
     * `Allocator.active()` does not bear on this. It drops a vshard past *half* the 9 GB VSHARD_CAP,
     * but `usage` is keyed by vshard and 384 of them hold 0.27 GiB apiece, so the active set stays
     * whole and `place()` keeps dividing by 384 throughout. Nothing sheds mid-load either:
     * `rebalance()` is an RPC that nothing calls, and the directory's alarm only resumes a move that
     * failed partway.
     */
    "HLEKK_VSHARDS": "256"
  },
  "observability": { "enabled": true }
}
